WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.731
Welcome to Module 4 of the
Ticket-to-Machine learning path:

2
00:00:03.731 --> 00:00:05.725
sources and the starting drift.

3
00:00:05.725 --> 00:00:09.975
Before you fix anything, you need to
know how far the directory is from HR.

4
00:00:09.975 --> 00:00:14.288
This module follows the guide's
Gate 3, which connects the sources,

5
00:00:14.288 --> 00:00:19.239
correlates them on one key, and files
the first measurement as the baseline,

6
00:00:19.239 --> 00:00:21.875
and the developer workbook's Chapter 06.

7
00:00:21.875 --> 00:00:26.970
You'll run the kit's read-only drift
reconciliation over two days of fictional data,

8
00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:29.967
compare the runs as new,
persisting and resolved,

9
00:00:29.967 --> 00:00:34.740
and see the comparison refuse two
runs that measured different things.

10
00:00:38.490 --> 00:00:39.728
Why measure first?

11
00:00:39.728 --> 00:00:44.778
Because drift comes in kinds, and each kind
needs a number before it needs a ticket.

12
00:00:44.778 --> 00:00:49.360
A terminated employee who's still
enabled in Entra ID is stale access,

13
00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:53.540
which the Federal Modernization
Workbook's Book 03 rates High.

14
00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:57.527
An account that matches nobody
is an orphan account, also High.

15
00:00:57.527 --> 00:01:02.165
A department someone typed in by hand
is attribute drift, rated Medium.

16
00:01:02.165 --> 00:01:04.968
Without a measurement you
can't say how many there are,

17
00:01:04.968 --> 00:01:07.452
whether they're growing,
or whether a fix worked.

18
00:01:07.452 --> 00:01:11.423
So Gate 3 connects the sources,
correlates them on one key,

19
00:01:11.423 --> 00:01:14.802
and files the first
measurement as the baseline.

20
00:01:14.802 --> 00:01:17.364
Every later gate is measured against it.

21
00:01:17.364 --> 00:01:22.660
In this module you run that measurement
twice, on two days, and compare the days.

22
00:01:26.410 --> 00:01:29.225
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

23
00:01:29.225 --> 00:01:31.848
which takes about forty-five
minutes with the lab.

24
00:01:31.848 --> 00:01:34.156
You'll explain what Gate 3 connects,

25
00:01:34.156 --> 00:01:37.897
the key it correlates on, and
what it files as the baseline.

26
00:01:37.897 --> 00:01:43.172
You'll name Book 03's drift classes, and
say which ones the kit doesn't compute.

27
00:01:43.172 --> 00:01:46.587
You'll run the drift
reconciliation over fixtures

28
00:01:46.587 --> 00:01:50.084
and compare two runs as new,
persisting and resolved.

29
00:01:50.084 --> 00:01:52.601
And you'll explain why
a comparison of runs

30
00:01:52.601 --> 00:01:55.260
that computed different
classes is refused.

31
00:01:55.260 --> 00:01:57.198
You need Module 3 first.

32
00:01:57.198 --> 00:02:00.145
For the Graph reads,
the Microsoft Automation

33
00:02:00.145 --> 00:02:03.559
path's first module covers
the four request patterns.

34
00:02:03.559 --> 00:02:09.620
Lab 1 runs on fixtures; Lab 2 needs a
test tenant and a SailPoint sandbox.

35
00:02:13.370 --> 00:02:15.195
Gate 3 has six steps.

36
00:02:15.195 --> 00:02:19.681
One: aggregate HR into SailPoint
as the authoritative source,

37
00:02:19.681 --> 00:02:22.408
read-only under Gate 2's first pattern.

38
00:02:22.408 --> 00:02:26.844
Two: connect Entra ID and Active
Directory as SailPoint sources, the Entra

39
00:02:26.844 --> 00:02:31.570
connector and the Active Directory
connector through the virtual appliance.

40
00:02:31.570 --> 00:02:35.870
Three: correlate on employee
I D, the key Book 03 joins on,

41
00:02:35.870 --> 00:02:42.082
and treat uncorrelated accounts as orphan
candidates, not errors to suppress.

42
00:02:42.082 --> 00:02:47.244
Four: turn on Native Change Detection on
the Entra and Active Directory sources.

43
00:02:47.244 --> 00:02:50.714
It compares stored and newly
read data on aggregation,

44
00:02:50.714 --> 00:02:53.258
and fires one event per changed account.

45
00:02:53.258 --> 00:02:57.158
Five: run the kit's drift
reconciliation, read-only.

46
00:02:57.158 --> 00:03:00.219
And six: file the first
run as the baseline.

47
00:03:00.219 --> 00:03:03.332
Expect a burst of High
findings in the first month.

48
00:03:03.332 --> 00:03:06.540
Book 03 calls that the
measurement working.

49
00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:12.413
Here are Book 03's drift classes,

50
00:03:12.413 --> 00:03:16.740
the ones the finding contract
carries, and what the kit computes.

51
00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:21.003
Missing identity: an active HR
record with no matching account.

52
00:03:21.003 --> 00:03:25.465
Stale access: terminated in
HR, enabled in the directory.

53
00:03:25.465 --> 00:03:29.728
Orphan account: an enabled account
with no known employee I D.

54
00:03:29.728 --> 00:03:32.603
Those three are High, and
the kit computes them.

55
00:03:32.603 --> 00:03:37.465
Attribute drift is Medium, and the
kit compares department and manager.

56
00:03:37.465 --> 00:03:41.853
Unauthorized write, a write by
anyone but the provisioning service,

57
00:03:41.853 --> 00:03:45.928
is High, and it's computed only
when the run reads the Entra audit.

58
00:03:45.928 --> 00:03:50.309
Credential drift and schema drift
are always reported as not computed,

59
00:03:50.309 --> 00:03:54.541
because they need the card system,
Intune and the middleware's output.

60
00:03:54.541 --> 00:03:59.020
The severities are the program's
defaults, not an external standard.

61
00:04:02.770 --> 00:04:05.482
The reconciliation is the kit's Build 3.

62
00:04:05.482 --> 00:04:10.557
It reads an HR extract, a C S V where
employee I D and status are required;

63
00:04:10.557 --> 00:04:14.117
Graph users with their
employee I D, enabled flag,

64
00:04:14.117 --> 00:04:19.457
department and manager's employee I
D; SailPoint identities from Search

65
00:04:19.457 --> 00:04:24.620
and uncorrelated accounts; and,
optionally, the Entra directory audit.

66
00:04:24.620 --> 00:04:27.533
In the labs each one is a saved export.

67
00:04:27.533 --> 00:04:31.987
Each finding's I D is a hash of
its class, system, employee I D,

68
00:04:31.987 --> 00:04:37.390
object I D and attribute, so the same
drift gets the same I D on every run,

69
00:04:37.390 --> 00:04:41.182
and the PowerShell client
produces the same I Ds.

70
00:04:41.182 --> 00:04:46.544
It writes JSON and C S V, saying which
classes were and weren't computed.

71
00:04:46.544 --> 00:04:49.107
Why run it at all, when events exist?

72
00:04:49.107 --> 00:04:53.799
Events catch changes as they happen;
the reconciliation catches the state,

73
00:04:53.799 --> 00:04:56.660
including drift whose event was missed.

74
00:05:00.410 --> 00:05:03.652
A scheduled run should
raise only what's new,

75
00:05:03.652 --> 00:05:06.985
so Chapter 06 adds a
comparison by finding I D.

76
00:05:06.985 --> 00:05:08.160
New means raise it.

77
00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:12.822
Persisting means it's already raised,
and the de-duplication adds a counter.

78
00:05:12.822 --> 00:05:15.060
Resolved means it's no longer measured.

79
00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:17.780
That's a lead for the
re-read, not a closure:

80
00:05:17.780 --> 00:05:21.160
its record still closes
only on the re-read client.

81
00:05:21.160 --> 00:05:24.932
And the comparison refuses runs
that computed different classes,

82
00:05:24.932 --> 00:05:26.852
one with the audit and one without,

83
00:05:26.852 --> 00:05:31.310
because every finding of the class the
second run skipped would look resolved.

84
00:05:31.310 --> 00:05:34.122
The lab's day-two fixture
tells a small story.

85
00:05:34.122 --> 00:05:37.640
HR corrects Erin's department
from HR to Finance,

86
00:05:37.640 --> 00:05:41.773
the value someone had already
typed into Entra ID by hand.

87
00:05:41.773 --> 00:05:43.749
Graph now agrees with HR.

88
00:05:43.749 --> 00:05:47.360
SailPoint, not yet re-aggregated, doesn't.

89
00:05:51.110 --> 00:05:55.548
Chapter 06's dangers are worth
reading before you schedule anything.

90
00:05:55.548 --> 00:05:58.021
A class one run skipped looks resolved:

91
00:05:58.021 --> 00:06:02.197
unauthorized writes disappear on
the night the audit read fails.

92
00:06:02.197 --> 00:06:04.460
The guard is the comparison's refusal.

93
00:06:04.460 --> 00:06:06.990
A resolved finding closes the record:

94
00:06:06.990 --> 00:06:11.398
drift that left the measurement,
not the estate, gets called fixed.

95
00:06:11.398 --> 00:06:14.085
The guard is that only the re-read closes.

96
00:06:14.085 --> 00:06:18.302
Pushing every finding every night
fills the tracker with repeats,

97
00:06:18.302 --> 00:06:20.985
so push only the
comparison's new findings.

98
00:06:20.985 --> 00:06:23.505
The first month's burst
gets read as failure,

99
00:06:23.505 --> 00:06:26.235
and the pipeline is turned
off before it's tuned.

100
00:06:26.235 --> 00:06:31.248
Book 03 expects that burst, so
triage it by class and severity.

101
00:06:31.248 --> 00:06:35.785
And an HR extract with names in a
shared folder spreads personal data.

102
00:06:35.785 --> 00:06:40.490
The run folder is evidence: keep
it where HR data is allowed.

103
00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:44.965
Try it.

104
00:06:44.965 --> 00:06:48.865
Lab 1 runs in the kit's folder
against fictional fixtures.

105
00:06:48.865 --> 00:06:53.365
Step one runs the drift and
comparison tests: seventeen pass.

106
00:06:53.365 --> 00:06:55.090
Step two runs day one.

107
00:06:55.090 --> 00:06:59.382
The summary counts four attribute
drifts, two missing identities,

108
00:06:59.382 --> 00:07:03.753
three orphan accounts, two stale
access and one unauthorized write,

109
00:07:03.753 --> 00:07:07.265
and lists credential and
schema drift as not computed.

110
00:07:07.265 --> 00:07:10.565
Then a one-line script prints
Erin's and Carol's rows.

111
00:07:10.565 --> 00:07:14.882
Erin's department: HR expects
HR, but Entra ID holds Finance,

112
00:07:14.882 --> 00:07:18.390
and the audit shows a
help-desk account wrote it.

113
00:07:18.390 --> 00:07:21.877
That's both attribute drift
and an unauthorized write.

114
00:07:21.877 --> 00:07:25.928
Her manager differs too, in
Entra ID and in SailPoint.

115
00:07:25.928 --> 00:07:29.890
Carol is terminated in HR,
but enabled in Entra ID

116
00:07:29.890 --> 00:07:34.310
and not disabled in SailPoint:
stale access, twice.

117
00:07:38.060 --> 00:07:41.797
Step three runs day two,
after HR's correction.

118
00:07:41.797 --> 00:07:45.660
The counts are exactly the same,
and the totals hide what moved.

119
00:07:45.660 --> 00:07:51.035
Step four compares the days: one new,
eleven persisting, one resolved.

120
00:07:51.035 --> 00:07:56.897
The resolved finding is Erin's department
in Entra ID, which now agrees with HR.

121
00:07:56.897 --> 00:08:00.122
It's a lead for the re-read,
and the tool says so:

122
00:08:00.122 --> 00:08:03.347
its record closes only
on the re-read client.

123
00:08:03.347 --> 00:08:06.433
The new finding is Erin's
department in SailPoint,

124
00:08:06.433 --> 00:08:10.197
which hasn't been re-aggregated
and still holds the old value.

125
00:08:10.197 --> 00:08:15.079
Step five runs day two without the audit,
so unauthorized write moves to not

126
00:08:15.079 --> 00:08:19.960
computed, and the comparison refuses:
the runs computed different classes.

127
00:08:19.960 --> 00:08:21.110
It exits two.

128
00:08:21.110 --> 00:08:25.272
Without that refusal, the unauthorized
write would have looked resolved.

129
00:08:25.272 --> 00:08:28.090
Step six deletes the output folder.

130
00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:33.765
Lab 2 runs the client live.

131
00:08:33.765 --> 00:08:37.854
You need a test Entra tenant
in the worldwide or GCC cloud,

132
00:08:37.854 --> 00:08:42.203
a SailPoint sandbox, and synthetic
HR data like the fixtures.

133
00:08:42.203 --> 00:08:45.715
Grant the read permissions from
Module 2, and nothing more.

134
00:08:45.715 --> 00:08:49.388
Write a synthetic HR extract
that matches your test users,

135
00:08:49.388 --> 00:08:54.515
run the client against the tenant and the
sandbox on two days, and compare the runs.

136
00:08:54.515 --> 00:08:56.190
Push nothing to ServiceNow.

137
00:08:56.190 --> 00:08:59.847
Two things the fixtures can't
prove are on the verify list:

138
00:08:59.847 --> 00:09:03.815
the manager expansion, and
SailPoint's employee I D attribute.

139
00:09:03.815 --> 00:09:08.600
Back it out in step five: delete the
output folder, the synthetic extract,

140
00:09:08.600 --> 00:09:13.952
the app registration's permissions and
certificate, and the sandbox API client.

141
00:09:13.952 --> 00:09:19.010
The authors haven't run Lab
2; follow Chapter 06's Lab 2.

142
00:09:22.760 --> 00:09:24.072
Where are agencies?

143
00:09:24.072 --> 00:09:30.560
Most on this path run Microsoft
365 GCC, and a minority GCC High.

144
00:09:30.560 --> 00:09:33.872
GCC tenants use the worldwide endpoints.

145
00:09:33.872 --> 00:09:39.126
In GCC High, you set the Entra source's
Graph and token endpoints to graph dot

146
00:09:39.126 --> 00:09:42.939
microsoft dot U S and login
dot microsoftonline dot U S;

147
00:09:42.939 --> 00:09:46.922
SailPoint's Entra connector
documents those overrides.

148
00:09:46.922 --> 00:09:49.035
One open question for every tenant:

149
00:09:49.035 --> 00:09:53.770
Native Change Detection's presence in
SailPoint's FedRAMP environment wasn't

150
00:09:53.770 --> 00:09:57.922
confirmed, which is one of the things
Gate 1 asks SailPoint in writing.

151
00:09:57.922 --> 00:10:01.851
The interim path: without SailPoint,
or before it's connected,

152
00:10:01.851 --> 00:10:06.251
run the kit's reconciliation against
the HR extract and Graph alone,

153
00:10:06.251 --> 00:10:09.472
because the SailPoint
inputs are optional files.

154
00:10:09.472 --> 00:10:13.070
Active Directory-only groups
wait for the appliance.

155
00:10:16.820 --> 00:10:19.758
Before you file the
baseline, validate the gate.

156
00:10:19.758 --> 00:10:21.557
The guide names four checks.

157
00:10:21.557 --> 00:10:24.730
The kit's tests prove the
logic against mocked data:

158
00:10:24.730 --> 00:10:26.845
the drift classes on the fixtures,

159
00:10:26.845 --> 00:10:30.622
the same finding I Ds from the
Python and PowerShell clients,

160
00:10:30.622 --> 00:10:35.607
and unauthorized writes reported as not
computed when there's no audit extract.

161
00:10:35.607 --> 00:10:38.345
Live, the reconciliation's findings on a

162
00:10:38.345 --> 00:10:41.682
synthetic HR extract
should match a hand count.

163
00:10:41.682 --> 00:10:45.531
The validation kit's two H R
checks look at the key itself:

164
00:10:45.531 --> 00:10:50.383
enabled members with no employee I D,
and duplicate employee I D values.

165
00:10:50.383 --> 00:10:53.510
Each should be clean, or each
row should have an owner,

166
00:10:53.510 --> 00:10:55.619
because the join depends on the key.

167
00:10:55.619 --> 00:10:58.879
And rollback is simple,
because the gate only reads:

168
00:10:58.879 --> 00:11:03.050
disconnect the source, or turn
off Native Change Detection.

169
00:11:06.800 --> 00:11:08.187
The federal requirement.

170
00:11:08.187 --> 00:11:12.738
The guide maps Gate 3 to NIST special
publication eight hundred fifty-three.

171
00:11:12.738 --> 00:11:16.624
A C two, account management: it
finds accounts with no worker,

172
00:11:16.624 --> 00:11:21.462
and workers with no account, and the
baseline drift report is the evidence.

173
00:11:21.462 --> 00:11:25.865
C A seven, continuous monitoring:
it starts a repeatable comparison

174
00:11:25.865 --> 00:11:30.188
of the record and the estate, kept
with its date and checker version.

175
00:11:30.188 --> 00:11:35.287
And, by inference, C M eight, read for
identities: one correlated inventory of

176
00:11:35.287 --> 00:11:36.685
accounts per person,

177
00:11:36.685 --> 00:11:41.126
shown by the correlation rate and
the uncorrelated-account list.

178
00:11:41.126 --> 00:11:46.215
Chapter 06 maps the reconciliation and
the comparison to account management,

179
00:11:46.215 --> 00:11:51.383
continuous monitoring and system monitoring,
and adds each run's JSON and C S V,

180
00:11:51.383 --> 00:11:56.270
with what wasn't computed, and the
comparison output kept with each run.

181
00:12:00.020 --> 00:12:03.520
Check yourself with two questions
from the module's knowledge check.

182
00:12:03.520 --> 00:12:08.595
First: on day two, Entra ID
matches HR for Erin's department.

183
00:12:08.595 --> 00:12:12.082
How does the comparison report
the day-one Entra finding?

184
00:12:12.082 --> 00:12:13.220
The answer is a.

185
00:12:13.220 --> 00:12:15.695
As resolved, a lead for the re-read.

186
00:12:15.695 --> 00:12:19.108
Its record still closes
only on the re-read client.

187
00:12:19.108 --> 00:12:22.344
A resolved finding is
a lead, not a closure.

188
00:12:22.344 --> 00:12:26.282
Second: why does the comparison
refuse to compare day one,

189
00:12:26.282 --> 00:12:28.795
with the audit, and day two, without it?

190
00:12:28.795 --> 00:12:30.020
The answer is b.

191
00:12:30.020 --> 00:12:33.343
Every unauthorized-write
finding would look resolved,

192
00:12:33.343 --> 00:12:36.233
because the second run
didn't compute that class.

193
00:12:36.233 --> 00:12:41.500
The module page has two more questions,
each linked to the section that answers it.

194
00:12:45.250 --> 00:12:46.075
To sum up.

195
00:12:46.075 --> 00:12:51.643
You learned how Gate 3 connects HR, Entra
ID and Active Directory through SailPoint,

196
00:12:51.643 --> 00:12:53.984
and correlates them on employee I D,

197
00:12:53.984 --> 00:12:57.938
with uncorrelated accounts
treated as orphan candidates.

198
00:12:57.938 --> 00:13:02.913
You named Book 03's drift classes,
and the two the kit never computes.

199
00:13:02.913 --> 00:13:05.099
You measured drift over two days,

200
00:13:05.099 --> 00:13:08.662
and compared the runs as new,
persisting and resolved.

201
00:13:08.662 --> 00:13:12.716
And you saw why the comparison refuses
runs that measured different things,

202
00:13:12.716 --> 00:13:16.062
so nothing looks fixed just
because it stopped being measured.

203
00:13:16.062 --> 00:13:19.616
Before you ship, work through
the Chapter 06 checklist,

204
00:13:19.616 --> 00:13:22.063
and file the first run as the baseline.

205
00:13:22.063 --> 00:13:26.322
Next is Module 5, drift to a typed
task: you'll predict the finding a

206
00:13:26.322 --> 00:13:31.280
SailPoint event produces, and test a
workflow change before you import it.
